-- title:
--   Descent of Uₚ and ⟨ d⟩ to the torus quotient
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero level $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$ (hypotheses `hpM`, `hpM2`, `hHp`), a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $Pl$ (the predicate `LiesOverPrime`) whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$, and the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`.
--
--   The geometric data are: an integral model package $\mathfrak{X}$ of type [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L81) for $X_H$ over $R_p =$ `R p`, level data $\Lambda$ and an object $O$ of type [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53), providing a smooth separated group object $O.G \to$ `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, an identification $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of $J_H =$ `JH M H` (the degree-zero divisor class group of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`) with its generic-fibre points, Hecke endomorphisms $O.\mathrm{hecke}$, a toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose point extends to the place) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ (generated by the range of the toric points). The representability hypotheses `hrep` and `hrepΛ` assert that the designations $(O.G, O.g)$ and $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$, with zero sections given by the units of $O.L$ and $\Lambda.L$, represent the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` for `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified by $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, respectively for `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` rigidified by $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   The base is a Henselian local domain $R_h$, faithfully embedded in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with `hRA` placing the image of $R_h$ inside $Pl$, `hRloc` identifying the maximal ideal of $R_h$ with the elements of valuation $< 1$, and an $R_h$-algebra structure on $\mathbb{Z}/p$ whose kernel is described by the same valuation condition (`hres`). Further data: a set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$.
--
--   The $p$-divisible group data consist of $\mathcal{G}$ of type [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199), a homomorphism $\Delta$ from the points of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $J_H$, and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between Tate modules, subject to the group of hypotheses `hΔinj`, `hΔlev`, `hΔgal`, `hΔhecke`, `he`, `heinj`, `herange`, `hegal`, `hsat`, `hcoker`, `htor`: $\Delta$ is injective; for each $v$ the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points is exactly $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$; $\Delta$ is equivariant for every $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and every $R_h$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing with $\tau$ pointwise; for every $S$ and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) there is a family of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ commuting with the transition maps and inducing `genOpH M H S g` on $\Delta$-images; $e$ is given componentwise by $\Delta$, is injective, has range the Tate-module elements whose $n$-th component lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$, is Galois-equivariant in the same sense, has saturated range ($p\,y$ in the range forces $y$ in the range), and has cokernel isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$ as a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-module; finally the toric points $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ lie in the image of the level-$v$ points under $\Delta$.
--
--   A second $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ of type [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) is given together with bialgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, numerical relations $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ and $h_B = 2h'$, and the group `hψt`, `hψker`, `hψsurj`, `hψred`, `hperiod`: the $\psi_v$ commute with the transition maps; precomposition with $\psi_v$ kills a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; precomposition with $\psi_v$ is surjective on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points; if the $\psi_v$-image of a point reduces trivially, in the sense that all values of its algebra map differ from the counit by an element of valuation $< 1$, then so does the point itself; and for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ in the degree-zero Picard group, and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ reduces trivially in that sense.
--
--   The comparison of $\mathcal{G}$ with $O.G$ is given by a ring homomorphism $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$, subject to the group `hρh`, `hιbase`, `hιcl`, `hιp`, `hιpts`, `hιmul`, `hιt`, `hιhecke`, `hιfin`: $\rho_h$ followed by the structure map of $R_h$ is the structure map of $R_p$ into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $R_h \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\mathrm{Spec}(\rho_h)$; the induced morphism to the fibre product is a closed immersion; $\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ for $O.L$ factors through the unit section; $\iota_v$ computes $O.\mathrm{pts}$ on $\Delta$-images of points; $\iota_v$ is additive for $O.L$ on points with values in any $R_h$-algebra $B$ (the two factorisation hypotheses being part of the statement); the $\iota_v$ are compatible with the transition maps; for every $S$ and every generator $g$ there is a family $\varphi_v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms commuting with the transitions, intertwining $\iota_v$ with $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,g$ and inducing `genOpH M H S g` on $\Delta$-images; and, for each $v$, the morphism $j_v$ obtained by lifting $\iota_v$ into the fibre product of the $p^v$-torsion of $O.G$ with $\mathrm{Spec}(\rho_h)$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that fibre product lying over the closed point of $R_h$ lies in the image of $j_v$.
--
--   The operators to be descended are a family $u_v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ and a family $D_{p,v}$ of $R_h$-bialgebra automorphisms, with: `hut` and `hDpt` (compatibility with the transition maps), `huι` (the $u_v$ intertwine $\iota_v$ with the Hecke operator $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S$ at the generator `CohCarrier.Gen.U p Fact.out hpM`), `hDpι` (the $D_{p,v}$ intertwine $\iota_v$ with $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S$ at the generator `CohCarrier.Gen.dia d`), and `hutor`, `hDptor` (both families preserve the property that the $\Delta$-image of a level-$v$ point lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$). On $\mathcal{B}$ there are families $u_{B,v}$ of bialgebra endomorphisms and $D_{B,v}$ of bialgebra automorphisms with $u_v \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ u_{B,v}$ and $D_{p,v} \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ D_{B,v}$ (`huB`, `hDB`).
--
--   Finally, $T$ is a family of commutative rings, each a finite free cocommutative Hopf algebra over $\mathbb{Z}/p$, together with $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra maps $\pi^T_v : \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to T_v$ which are surjective (`hπT`) and whose kernel, as an ideal, is the image under $\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes \psi_v$ of the augmentation ideal, i.e. of the kernel of the counit of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ (`hexT`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist two families $u^T_v, e^T_v$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra endomorphisms of $T_v$ and a family $D'_{B,v}$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p$-bialgebra self-equivalences of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ such that, for every $v$:
--
--   (i) $\pi^T_v \circ (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes u_v) = u^T_v \circ \pi^T_v$;
--
--   (ii) $\pi^T_v \circ (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes D_{p,v}) = e^T_v \circ \pi^T_v$;
--
--   (iii) $D'_{B,v}$, viewed as a bialgebra homomorphism, equals $\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes D_{B,v}$;
--
--   (iv) $(\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes u_v) \circ (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes \psi_v) = (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes \psi_v) \circ (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes u_{B,v})$;
--
--   (v) $(\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes D_{p,v}) \circ (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes \psi_v) = (\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes \psi_v) \circ D'_{B,v}$.
--
--   Here $\mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{Z}/p} \otimes f$ denotes the base change of $f$ along $R_h \to \mathbb{Z}/p$, formed by `Bialgebra.TensorProduct.map`.
--
--   This is the special-fibre half of the descent step in the construction of the two-step filtration of the finite part of the $p$-divisible group attached to $J_H$ at a prime dividing the level exactly once: the operators $U_p$ and $\langle d \rangle$, already realised on $\mathcal{G}$ and compatible with the Raynaud quotient $\mathcal{B}$, are carried across the surjections $\pi^T_v$ onto the quotient Hopf algebras $T_v$ over $\mathbb{F}_p$ computing the torus quotient. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_descent_torusQuotient_raynaudQuotient_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_injective`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_descent_torusQuotient_raynaudQuotient_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_injective), which assembles these descents with the remaining conjuncts of the filtration statement.
